    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d460">

                <pb facs="0722" n="606" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg606"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.113/437: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-02-09T18:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 9,
                                1923—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received February 9—6:05
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>46. Your 257 November 6, 6 p.m., 1922.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d460-f1"/>
                    Diplomatic body met today to consider first, arms embargo act regarding which
                    some ministers had only recently received instructions from their governments
                    and secondly proposal to withhold naval assistance to China.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>With regard to arms embargo Netherlands Minister expressed his objection
                        to the interpretation clause of the identic telegram contained in my 405
                        October 4, 11 a.m. 1922.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d460-f2"/> Other ministers
                        declared their governments would not join in the embargo unless all nations
                        did, and referred to the noninclusion of Russia, Finally the following
                        statement was unanimously agreed on as a substitute for the identic
                        telegram. <quote rend="blockquote">
                            <p>“The measures agreed to of [<hi rendition="#italic">by?</hi>] the
                                foreign representatives in China <hi rendition="#italic">re</hi>
                                embargo and [<hi rendition="#italic">on</hi>] arms would in the main
                                remain ineffective because the majority of the foreign
                                representatives have no legal power to enforce them. In view of
                                continued internal warfare they are however of opinion that as many
                                powers as possible should approve the resolution proposed at
                                    Washington<anchor n="32" xml:id="d460-f3"/> without reservations
                                but widening scope by including aircraft other than commercial
                                craft.”</p>
                        </quote></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>With regard to proposal to withhold naval assistance to China the
                        Netherlands Minister pointed out that this was matter which ultimately would
                        have to be decided by his Government and in this opinion others concurred.
                        The Japanese representative called attention to the change of language as
                        stated in Department’s telegram 15, January 24, 5 p.m.<anchor n="33"
                            xml:id="d460-f4"/> and said that his Government had not been notified of
                        such change. Whereon I suggested that the matter be laid over until the next
                        meeting.</item>
                </list>
                <p>A copy of this message has been mailed to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
